    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if i'm thinking about it in terms of the air being physically pushed, how does the shape of the wing actually influence that deflection? like why does the curve matter so much for the downward force part?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The curved shape, or camber, forces incoming airflow to follow a longer path over the top, causing it to turn more sharply downward than if it were flat. This sharp downward turn creates the necessary reaction force according to Newton's third law, directly generating lift. Without that curve and an appropriate angle of attack, the air would not be deflected significantly enough to produce the downward momentum change required for lift.",
      "source": "target",
