    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so basically the wing is just manipulating the air to create these forces. is there anything else that typically interferes with that flow or makes it less efficient? like, what are the main things that mess with getting that perfect downward deflection?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, several factors can disrupt that efficient airflow and reduce lift efficiency, primarily by preventing the smooth curvature of the air as intended. Drag, especially pressure drag caused by turbulence and form drag, fights directly against the momentum transfer needed to push air downward. Furthermore, if the airflow separates from the wing's surface due to too high a speed or angle, the smooth deflection breaks down entirely, causing a stall where both Bernoulli pressure differences and Newtonian reaction forces collapse. Consequently, engine thrust is often needed just to overcome this parasitic drag just to maintain speed, not just to provide lift.",
      "source": "target",
